         <span class="r">Model Rule 1.1</span>
         <h3>Competence</h3>
         <p>A lawyer must provide competent representation &mdash; the legal knowledge, skill, thoroughness, and preparation the matter requires. Comment 8 extends it to technology: keeping abreast of the benefits and risks of the tools of practice.</p>
-        <p class="bite"><b>Where it bites in research:</b> thoroughness is a research duty. Skipping the citator, stopping before the question is answered, or using an AI tool without understanding how it fails are all competence problems before they are anything else.</p>
+        <p class="bite"><b>Relevance to Research</b> Thoroughness is part of competent representation. This duty requires you to complete the research cycle, verify your authorities with a citator, and understand the limits of any tool you use before you rely on its results.</p>
       </div>
       <div class="duty">
         <span class="r">Model Rule 3.3</span>
         <h3>Candor to the tribunal</h3>
         <p>A lawyer may not knowingly make a false statement of law to a court, and must disclose controlling authority directly adverse to the client&rsquo;s position if opposing counsel has not.</p>
-        <p class="bite"><b>Where it bites in research:</b> twice. A citation you never verified can make you the one presenting false authority &mdash; and the adverse case your research surfaces is not optional to deal with. Finding it created the duty.</p>
+        <p class="bite"><b>Relevance to Research</b> This duty requires you to verify every citation before you present it to a court. It also requires you to address controlling adverse authority once your research finds it. You cannot set a case aside because it hurts your position.</p>
       </div>
       <div class="duty">
         <span class="r">Model Rule 1.6</span>
         <h3>Confidentiality</h3>
         <p>A lawyer must not reveal information relating to the representation, and must make reasonable efforts to prevent unauthorized disclosure.</p>
-        <p class="bite"><b>Where it bites in research:</b> the tools. Client facts typed into a free public AI tool, a search history on a shared account, a docket request that telegraphs a deal &mdash; research inputs can be disclosures.</p>
+        <p class="bite"><b>Relevance to Research</b> This duty requires you to consider what your research activity reveals. Entering client facts into a public AI tool, running searches on a shared account, or requesting a docket can each disclose information about the representation.</p>
       </div>
       <div class="duty">
         <span class="r">Model Rules 5.1 &amp; 5.3</span>
         <h3>Supervision</h3>
         <p>Lawyers with supervisory authority must make reasonable efforts to ensure that subordinate lawyers&rsquo; and nonlawyers&rsquo; work conforms to professional obligations.</p>
-        <p class="bite"><b>Where it bites in research:</b> the sign-off. A supervisor who files a junior&rsquo;s unverified memo owns the failure &mdash; and &ldquo;the associate did the research&rdquo; (or &ldquo;the AI did&rdquo;) has never been a defense.</p>
+        <p class="bite"><b>Relevance to Research</b> This duty requires you to verify the research of the people and tools working under you. A supervisor who files a subordinate&rsquo;s unverified memo is responsible for its errors, whether the work came from an associate or from an AI tool.</p>
       </div>
     </div>
 
